WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.830
Speaker 1: OK, I want to start today with
how to research a news story that you read

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:06.830
on the internet.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:12.700
I often talk about media literacy, critical
thinking, epistemology, understanding why

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.100
we believe the things we believe.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.610
And part of this as news consumers is, is
what I'm reading trustworthy.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.590
You can, I believe what I'm reading.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:24.300
So I'm going to do this in kind of order of
depth and time.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.300
So like, this is the order you could do it
in and you could stop at any time.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.159
This is this is going to be a lot of work
is the truth, and it's not realistic that

00:00:33.159 --> 00:00:37.129
every news consumer is going to do this level
of research on every single story.

00:00:37.129 --> 00:00:43.609
But the typical thing that happens is we have
an article, the article says something and

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:46.629
we need to figure out, can can we trust it?

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:52.219
And this is to be very clear, not how you
would do academic research.

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:56.899
I know some people will say, David, this is
not how academics research academic topics.

00:00:56.899 --> 00:00:57.899
No, no, no.

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:01.180
I'm just saying this is something the average
news consumer can do.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:06.229
If you want to at least have a framework for
figuring out, can I believe what I'm reading?

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:12.000
So I deliberately picked an article from the
website Raw Story to start with.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.650
I'm not criticizing Raw Story.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:18.909
I'm just using Raw Story because it's an article
that is emblematic of so much of what there

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:21.369
is on the internet, which is it's basically
a rewrite.

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:23.659
It's not a primary source.

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:25.660
This is an article based on another article.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.359
OK, so let's start here and we'll go and you.

00:01:28.359 --> 00:01:30.450
You could stop anywhere, but this is the order.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.149
I'd suggest you're presented with an article.

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:37.380
The article is from Raw Story, and it says
New York Lieutenant Governor Brian Benjamin

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.929
arrested on federal bribery charges.

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:45.770
OK, now you start reading through the article
and you will realize, Oh, this is not original

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:46.770
reporting.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.979
This is a rewrite of an original article.

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:54.439
In this case, it's really easy to tell because
the first line is.

00:01:54.439 --> 00:01:59.109
The New York Times reported that New York
Lieutenant Governor Brian Benjamin has surrendered

00:01:59.109 --> 00:02:01.289
to federal authorities on bribery charges.

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:07.210
Now you can finish reading this article if
you want, or you could go right to the primary

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:08.260
source.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:12.580
So we go to the primary source, which is the
New York Times article.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:13.580
Great.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.450
So you can read this article.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.840
This would be the next thing to do.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.760
Lieutenant Governor Benjamin resigns following
campaign finance indictment.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.280
Brian Benjamin, New York's second in command
to the governor, was charged with bribery.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.380
OK, so you read this article?

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.380
Great.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:35.481
Now one thing that is useful to do in assessing
media bias would be, well, this is a New York

00:02:35.481 --> 00:02:37.630
Times article.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.570
What's the deal with The New York Times?

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:44.820
We all sort of know The New York Times, but
imagine that it's a less well-known primary

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:45.820
source.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:51.230
But the the next thing that you would do is
you would Google the name of the media outlet

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:55.750
and the word bias or the word source watch.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:00.790
If there's a source watch page that can be
useful and you will find websites that will

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.540
assess the credibility of the source.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.400
In the case of the New York Times, there's
many all sides dot com.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.440
We'll talk to you about the New York Times,
and it says the New York Times leans left,

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:18.480
and it gives you information about how that
was determined, and it tells you different

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.260
things about the New York Times.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:30.630
The ad fund has media page will have a page
about the New York Times bias and reliability

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:37.090
source watch for some more prominent media
outlets will tell you about what is The New

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.090
York Times?

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:39.610
Again, New York Times.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.010
Most of us don't need to do this, but you
might come across a source and you don't know

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:48.340
if it's credible, so you would want to after
finding the primary source.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.750
Learn a little bit about the media outlet.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:51.750
Great.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.460
Now sometimes it's about the author of the
article.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:59.320
The author themselves might be notable.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:04.380
In this case, it's a group of authors from
the New York Times William Ramsbottom, Nicholas

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.760
Van Doce and Jeffrey Mayes.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:12.720
If Bob Woodward is the author, the author
has a multi-decade history, and you can examine

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.950
that to kind of build your assessment of the
of the story.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.660
These are less well-known authors.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:21.690
And so just as an example.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:26.750
William Rasch bomb is not notable enough to
have his own wiki page, but you can at least

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:30.640
look at the William Rash Bomb page on the
New York Times, and it tells you this is a

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.690
senior writer, metro staff.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.670
He does political and municipal corruption.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:38.730
So OK, this is not like a culture writer writing
about political corruption.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:44.140
It's a political corruption story, and that's
what this particular journalist writes about.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:45.970
He used to cover federal courts in Brooklyn.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.440
He was the newspaper's police bureau chief.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:49.440
Great.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.440
OK.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:54.250
So we have a bunch of information about William
Rash Bomb, and he seems well-suited based

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:57.500
on his experience to write about this type
of thing.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:58.500
Great.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.150
OK, so we could stop here and we could say,
like, this is it?

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:09.240
This is a it's an author who knows about this
topic in a generally reliable media outlet.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.650
We're comfortable.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:14.290
OK, now you could go further.

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:18.450
This is an article about federal bribery.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:24.440
So you could actually say, Well, let me look
at the underlying law, and Cornell has a great

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:29.170
website where you can look up any law and
statute and you can say, let me learn what

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:33.240
is in the bribery of public officials and
witnesses.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:37.400
Federal statute, because that's what this
entire story is based on.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.400
Great.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.590
Well, I might also want to know the history
of such charges.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:43.950
Who else has been charged with federal bribery?

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:45.450
Who's been convicted?

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.130
Is there a political nature to these types
of charges?

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:52.820
So there is a wiki page called List of United
States Officials.

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:58.220
The list of United States state officials
convicted of federal corruption charges, which

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.800
includes bribery.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.639
Now you might say well, but David, Wikipedia
is not a good source.

00:06:03.639 --> 00:06:09.080
Wikipedia is a great aggregation when there
are citations because you can start looking

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.840
through this list and you see all these different
people.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:17.370
Governor Rod Blagojevich and many others and
their citations for all of these, you can

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:18.490
actually look at notes.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:23.730
And if you say, Oh, OK, well, what was the
deal with Rod Blagojevich's conviction?

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:26.550
So it's actually very, very useful for that.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:31.450
And then also, you know, it could be useful
again, diving deeper to look at information

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:34.260
about the person that the article is about.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:39.520
This entire story is about the lieutenant
governor, Brian Benjamin.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.490
So you might start with their wiki page.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:45.770
And again, you're not using Wiki so much as
a source.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:51.620
You're using it as a kind of clearinghouse
for citations, and you could learn about Brian

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:52.949
Benjamin's history.

00:06:52.949 --> 00:06:56.520
Does he have a history of legal trouble?

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:57.520
On and on.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:58.900
OK, so so you would go from there?

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:03.210
You could find kind of key information about
the person in their bio.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.490
That's not in the article.

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:10.460
So this is like the minimum to really more
deeply start understanding a topic and knowing

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:16.460
whether remember, we started with a rewrite
on Raw Story and compare what you would know

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:21.130
just reading the Raw Story article versus
actually doing this work now.

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:23.150
This is just a frame.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:24.520
Imagine it's a science story.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:30.580
Well, that's arguably more complex, imagine
that we're looking at an article about anti-nuclear

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:37.020
activists are warning about building new nuclear,
well, OK, that's a lot.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:39.199
So is it the original source or not?

00:07:39.199 --> 00:07:43.980
We would want to go to the primary source,
but nuclear power is a major academic topic,

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:50.020
so we might even go to scholars google.com
and search for nuclear power.

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:55.220
Nuclear safety differences between 40 year
old nuclear power and modern nuclear power.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:57.729
OK, so so it takes work.

00:07:57.729 --> 00:08:04.229
And understandably, most people don't have
time to do this level of investigation.

00:08:04.229 --> 00:08:06.300
For every news story you get.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:11.580
But it should at least let you know that reading
a piece on Raw Story is not going to leave

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:19.010
you informed in a way that I would like to
be if I'm going to be talking about a topic.

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:25.350
Keep in mind when the anti-vaxxers say, I've
done my research, think about what research

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:30.300
they may have done, and you can actually use
this framework to inquire and probe whether

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.419
they've really actually done their research.

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:35.060
So hopefully this is useful.

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:36.060
Let me know.

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:37.539
Maybe at some point I'll do another example.

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:42.699
You know, I deliberately picked a pretty cut
and dry story which just like a guy, got arrested.

00:08:42.699 --> 00:08:45.690
And here's here's why.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:47.290
To show the depth you can go in.

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:51.380
Even for that, if this is useful, I'm glad
to glad to do it again with other topics

